<%# locals: (turn:, playthrough: nil, verdicts: {}) %>
<%# ONE TURN OF THE LOG, IN ONE WRAPPER.

    `.entry` exists so a turn's prose and the machinery panel beside it are one
    thing the stylesheet can lay out in two columns on a wide viewport -- the
    captain's ask of 2026-09-05, *"I can't compare the two side by side"*. It is
    also what the dimming rule now counts: `.log:not(.streaming) > .entry:last-of-type
    .turn`, which is the same behaviour the old `.log > .turn:last-of-type` had
    and is no longer sensitive to what tag anything under a turn happens to use.
    Four tests assert that selector directly. %>
<div class="entry">
  <div class="said">
    <%# A talk turn keeps an Interaction alongside the Scene, so the log can say
        who the player was speaking to. Narrated and arrival turns have none. %>
    <% speakers = turn.interactions.map(&:character_name).uniq %>
    <% if speakers.any? %>
      <p class="speaking">talking to <%= speakers.to_sentence %></p>
    <% end %>
    <div class="turn"><%= turn.description %></div>
    <%# THE VERDICT ON THIS TURN, and half of the instrument's mark on the game.
        Gated on `Playthrough::Debug.enabled?` -- the same flag as the debug link
        and the machinery panel below, local by default -- so someone handed a
        playthrough link to read a story is never shown it. See
        FeedbacksController for why they share a gate.

        `verdicts` is one query for the whole log (`Playthrough#feedback_by_scene`),
        not a lookup per entry: the log is the entire playthrough. %>
    <% if playthrough && Playthrough::Debug.enabled? %>
      <%= render "feedbacks/verdict", playthrough: playthrough, scene: turn,
                                      feedback: local_assigns.fetch(:verdicts, {})[turn.id] %>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%# THE MACHINERY BEHIND THIS TURN: the state it was played in and the prompt
      it was written from, beside the prose rather than a page away.

      CALLED "machinery" AND NOT "inspect", deliberately: `/inspect` is a verb in
      the game since the slash menu gained it, and a control sharing that word
      would leave a player unable to tell inspecting the ward stamp from
      inspecting the turn that described it.

      GATED, AND NOT RENDERED AT ALL WHEN THE FLAG IS OFF. The endpoint is gated
      too (`MachineryController`) -- this app has no auth, and what it answers
      with is the prompt text.

      LAZY, ONE TURN AT A TIME. The frame carries `loading="lazy"` inside a
      CLOSED `<details>`: a frame that is not visible does not fetch, so a log of
      forty turns costs forty bytes of markup and no requests until one is
      opened. That matters because `#turn_log` is replaced by a Turbo Stream at
      the end of every turn and the log is the whole playthrough.

      IT CLOSES WHEN A TURN LANDS, and that is the accepted cost of holding no
      state anywhere: the replace re-renders every entry out of the records, so a
      panel that was open comes back closed. Nothing is lost but the click, and
      the alternative is either draft state in JavaScript or narrowing what the
      broadcast replaces -- and `play_controller`'s scroll and `preventScroll`
      refocus were found in a browser and are not worth risking for it.

      WITH NO JAVASCRIPT the frame never fetches, and the placeholder inside it
      is an ordinary link to the same action, which renders the panel under the
      game's own layout. %>
  <% if playthrough && Playthrough::Debug.enabled? %>
    <details class="machinery">
      <summary>machinery</summary>
      <turbo-frame id="<%= dom_id(turn, :machinery) %>" loading="lazy"
                   src="<%= playthrough_machinery_path(playthrough, turn) %>">
        <p class="sheet-note">
          <%= link_to "read the records for this turn",
                      playthrough_machinery_path(playthrough, turn) %>
        </p>
      </turbo-frame>
    </details>
  <% end %>
</div>
